      "title": "Why Onions Make You Cry",
      "source": "예시 글",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Cutting an onion often brings tears, even to people who never cry at movies. When a knife breaks the onion's cells, it {{releases|release}} chemicals that mix and form a gas. This gas floats up and reaches your eyes, and your eyes {{respond|respond}} by producing tears to wash it away.",
        "Chilling the onion before cutting it can slow the reaction, and a sharp knife damages fewer cells. Some scientists have even developed tearless onions through careful breeding. Still, many cooks simply {{accept|accept}} a few tears as part of the job."
      ],
